  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Jesus (c. 6–4 BC – AD 30–33) was a 1st-century Jewish preacher and central figure of Christianity. Revered by Christians as the Son of God and Messiah, he taught love and forgiveness, and his life, death by crucifixion, and reported resurrection form the core of Christian faith and offer salvation. Key Aspects of Jesus's Life and Role Birth: Born to the Virgin Mary in Bethlehem of Judea, often commemorated at Christmas. Ministry: Began around age 30, baptized by John the Baptist, and involved teaching through parables and performing miracles, largely in Galilee and Judea. Role in Christianity: Christians believe Jesus is God incarnate (fully divine and fully human), who atoned for human sin through his death and resurrection. Resurrection: Christians believe he died on the cross and rose from the dead, offering eternal life. Names and Titles: Known as Jesus of Nazareth, Jesus Christ (\"the anointed one\"), and the Messiah. Historical Existence: Almost all modern scholars agree that Jesus was a historical figure who lived in the Roman province of Judaea. Key TeachingsJesus emphasized a personal relationship with God over strict, ritualistic adherence to law, stating that the law is fulfilled through love, faith, and the inner state of the heart. Key Events Birth (c. 6-4 BC) Baptism and 40 days in the desert Public ministry and choosing 12 disciples The Last Supper Crucifixion (c. AD 30 or 33) Resurrection and Ascension",
